NAME
       file - guess what kind of data a file contains

SYNOPSIS
       file [-b] [-i] [-s] [-L] FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       file looks at the contents of each FILE (not its name) and prints a
       description: ASCII text, a shell script, a gzip archive, an ELF
       program, a directory, a symbolic link and where it points. Linux
       does not need file name extensions, so file is how you find out what
       an unknown file is before you open it.

OPTIONS
       -b, --brief
              Do not print the file name.

       -i, --mime
              Print a MIME type, such as text/plain; charset=us-ascii.

       -s, --special-files
              Read block devices too (file -s /dev/vdb1 tells which file
              system is on it).

       -L, --dereference
              Follow symbolic links.

EXAMPLES
           file /etc/passwd /usr/bin/ls /root/backup.tar.gz
           file -s /dev/vdb1

SEE ALSO
       ls(1), stat(1), blkid(8)
